Yes. And I see the larger principle you’re pointing toward.

A sense isn’t necessarily defined by what it detects. It’s defined by how information from the world gets transduced, represented, integrated, and interpreted.

You’re describing something that extends beyond the five senses into a much broader architecture of perception.

An X-ray, an ultrasound, a camera, an ear, and a pressure sensor can all provide information about the same object or environment. But each interacts with reality differently, revealing features that the others cannot.

One technical distinction: X-rays are electromagnetic photons, like visible light but at much higher energies. Ultrasound uses mechanical pressure waves traveling through a medium, not photons. The distinction actually strengthens your point: radically different physical carriers can produce representations of the same underlying reality.

1. Reality doesn’t arrive in one format

X-ray

Differences in X-ray attenuation reveal internal structures.

Ultrasound

Reflections of sound waves reveal boundaries and movement.

Thermal imaging

Infrared emissions provide information about surface temperature.

LiDAR

Reflected laser light enables geometric reconstruction.

Each is a different measurement operator on the world.

Yet all four can be converted into something a human can see on a screen.

That means visible images are not the only source of visualizable information. They’re one possible interface for representing measurements that originate in completely different physical processes.

And the conversion isn’t limited to images. We can represent distance through vibration, temperature through sound, or pressure through color.

That’s sensory substitution and cross-modal representation.

2. The real operation is sensor fusion

What you’re describing is closely related to multisensory integration, sensor fusion, and cross-modal inference.

The brain doesn’t simply receive a picture, a sound, and a sensation and stack them together. It must determine which signals correspond to the same external event, align their different reference frames, and decide how reliable they are. Research on multisensory perception describes precisely these operations. PubMed Central (PMC) +1

