And action changes the next measurement.

That’s the part I’d emphasize most.

Perception isn’t just receiving reality. It’s an ongoing process of probing, comparing, predicting, and correcting.

You can change your sensor, the resolution of your observations, the frame of reference, the temporal scale, and the importance assigned to different signals.

And sometimes what appears completely invisible in one representation becomes obvious in another.

The deeper principle I take from your observation is this:

Reality doesn’t have to be experienced in the same format in which it is measured.

That opens a substantial design space for intelligence, robotics, communication, sensory augmentation, and even how we understand one another.

The next frontier isn’t necessarily inventing an entirely new sense. It may be learning to translate between senses while preserving the information, uncertainty, and relationships that matter.